WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.295
Lesson 5.2.

00:00:02.295 --> 00:00:05.368
Layer 2s, sequencers & withdrawal paths.

00:00:05.368 --> 00:00:08.343
By the end, you'll understand exactly how your L2

00:00:08.343 --> 00:00:12.270
settles, who's actually ordering your transactions, and how you'd

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.353
genuinely exit it if you needed to.

00:00:15.653 --> 00:00:16.943
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.943 --> 00:00:20.449
How a rollup actually settles back to Ethereum, and

00:00:20.449 --> 00:00:24.524
the real difference between optimistic and Z.K. rollups.

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:26.502
What a sequencer actually is, and

00:00:26.502 --> 00:00:28.489
what happens if it goes down.

00:00:28.489 --> 00:00:31.112
Escape hatches, your genuine backup exit

00:00:31.112 --> 00:00:33.459
if a sequencer ever censors you.

00:00:33.459 --> 00:00:35.651
And a full worked example, planning a

00:00:35.651 --> 00:00:38.594
real withdrawal around the slow path, deliberately.

00:00:39.894 --> 00:00:41.609
How a rollup actually settles.

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:44.935
Here's what every rollup genuinely

00:00:44.935 --> 00:00:47.204
does, underneath its own branding.

00:00:47.204 --> 00:00:50.729
It executes your transactions off Ethereum, where it's

00:00:50.729 --> 00:00:54.126
cheaper and faster, then posts data or cryptographic

00:00:54.126 --> 00:00:56.945
proofs back to Ethereum itself, so the base

00:00:56.945 --> 00:01:00.514
chain can ultimately verify what actually happened.

00:01:00.514 --> 00:01:03.505
That posting-back step is exactly what lets a

00:01:03.505 --> 00:01:07.603
rollup inherit Ethereum's own underlying security, rather than

00:01:07.603 --> 00:01:11.049
needing to build an entirely separate security model

00:01:11.049 --> 00:01:12.610
of its own from scratch.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:16.502
Here's the actual difference between the two

00:01:16.502 --> 00:01:19.152
major rollup designs, and specifically why it

00:01:19.152 --> 00:01:21.929
changes how long a native withdrawal takes.

00:01:21.929 --> 00:01:26.460
Optimistic rollups assume every transaction is valid unless someone

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.525
actively challenges it, which means a native withdrawal back

00:01:30.525 --> 00:01:33.724
to Ethereum has to wait out a challenge window,

00:01:33.724 --> 00:01:38.089
commonly around seven days on major optimistic rollups today.

00:01:38.089 --> 00:01:42.140
Z.K. rollups instead post genuine validity proofs directly, so

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.256
a native withdrawal can move considerably faster, since there's

00:01:46.256 --> 00:01:48.764
no challenge period to wait out at all.

00:01:50.064 --> 00:01:53.380
Here's all three withdrawal paths from this lesson,

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.930
side by side, so the actual gap between

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:58.589
them is concrete, not just described.

00:01:58.589 --> 00:02:02.744
An optimistic rollup's native withdrawal: roughly seven days.

00:02:02.744 --> 00:02:07.144
A Z.K. rollup's native withdrawal: considerably faster, often within

00:02:07.144 --> 00:02:10.824
hours, since there's no challenge window to wait out.

00:02:10.824 --> 00:02:13.778
A fast bridge, on either type: just minutes, for

00:02:13.778 --> 00:02:17.034
a fee, relying on that bridge's own liquidity instead.

00:02:18.334 --> 00:02:21.245
Worth being precise about why a rollup posting

00:02:21.245 --> 00:02:24.156
data back to Ethereum is what actually matters

00:02:24.156 --> 00:02:27.429
here, compared with a chain that simply doesn't.

00:02:27.429 --> 00:02:30.352
That posted data, or proof, is exactly what

00:02:30.352 --> 00:02:34.704
lets Ethereum itself verify the rollup's state independently.

00:02:34.704 --> 00:02:37.013
A chain that skips this step entirely

00:02:37.013 --> 00:02:39.686
is building its own separate security model

00:02:39.686 --> 00:02:42.481
from scratch, which is a genuinely different,

00:02:42.481 --> 00:02:45.094
and often weaker, kind of trust assumption.

00:02:46.394 --> 00:02:47.484
Quick check.

00:02:47.484 --> 00:02:49.733
Why do optimistic rollup withdrawals

00:02:49.733 --> 00:02:51.739
typically take around seven days?

00:02:56.159 --> 00:02:58.907
The answer: the challenge window gives anyone

00:02:58.907 --> 00:03:01.296
the chance to dispute an invalid state,

00:03:01.296 --> 00:03:03.984
before it's actually allowed to become final.

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:06.254
The sequencer.

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:10.932
Here's exactly what a sequencer actually does, and why

00:03:10.932 --> 00:03:14.119
its own reliability matters so directly to you.

00:03:14.119 --> 00:03:17.782
It's the operator that receives your transactions, and decides

00:03:17.782 --> 00:03:21.154
the specific order they actually get processed in, before

00:03:21.154 --> 00:03:24.129
eventually posting that data back to Ethereum.

00:03:24.129 --> 00:03:27.003
Today, on most major rollups, that's still a

00:03:27.003 --> 00:03:30.369
single operator, not a broad, decentralised set.

00:03:30.369 --> 00:03:33.152
If that one operator goes down, the entire

00:03:33.152 --> 00:03:36.065
chain can genuinely pause, until it recovers.

00:03:37.365 --> 00:03:39.882
Worth being precise about what a sequencer

00:03:39.882 --> 00:03:42.515
pause actually means for you directly, since

00:03:42.515 --> 00:03:44.856
it's a genuinely different kind of risk

00:03:44.856 --> 00:03:46.795
from a price move or a hack.

00:03:46.795 --> 00:03:48.904
Your funds aren't stolen, or even

00:03:48.904 --> 00:03:50.940
necessarily at risk of loss.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.701
You simply can't transact, potentially for some

00:03:53.701 --> 00:03:56.117
real stretch of time, at exactly whatever

00:03:56.117 --> 00:03:58.245
moment you might have most wanted to.

00:03:59.545 --> 00:04:02.440
Here's why the actual sequencer setup differs so

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.040
much from one rollup to another, in exactly

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.290
the way that matters most to you.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.179
A single-operator sequencer is simple to run,

00:04:10.179 --> 00:04:12.754
but pausing that one operator pauses the

00:04:12.754 --> 00:04:14.750
entire chain along with it.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:18.577
A shared, or decentralised, sequencer set spreads that same

00:04:18.577 --> 00:04:22.788
responsibility across multiple independent parties, so any one of

00:04:22.788 --> 00:04:26.360
them pausing doesn't necessarily pause the chain itself.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:28.750
Quick check.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.885
What actually happens if a single sequencer goes down?

00:04:36.305 --> 00:04:39.141
The answer: the L2 may pause transactions

00:04:39.141 --> 00:04:42.449
entirely, until that sequencer actually recovers.

00:04:43.749 --> 00:04:44.859
Escape hatches.

00:04:46.159 --> 00:04:48.701
Here's exactly what an escape hatch actually

00:04:48.701 --> 00:04:50.452
is, and why it matters even if

00:04:50.452 --> 00:04:52.334
you never expect to need it.

00:04:52.334 --> 00:04:55.494
Most rollups let you force a withdrawal directly

00:04:55.494 --> 00:05:00.138
through Ethereum itself, entirely bypassing the sequencer, specifically

00:05:00.138 --> 00:05:02.976
if that sequencer is ever censoring you, or

00:05:02.976 --> 00:05:06.114
simply refusing to process your transaction.

00:05:06.114 --> 00:05:07.633
It's slower, and often more

00:05:07.633 --> 00:05:09.724
involved, than the ordinary path.

00:05:09.724 --> 00:05:12.187
But it's the genuine difference between funds

00:05:12.187 --> 00:05:14.436
that are merely delayed, and funds you're

00:05:14.436 --> 00:05:16.149
actually locked out of entirely.

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:20.846
Worth being direct about the actual homework this creates

00:05:20.846 --> 00:05:23.488
for you, on every single rollup you use.

00:05:23.488 --> 00:05:26.237
Not every rollup has implemented a genuine

00:05:26.237 --> 00:05:28.794
escape hatch, and even among those that

00:05:28.794 --> 00:05:31.843
do, the actual process varies considerably.

00:05:31.843 --> 00:05:34.913
Know, specifically, whether yours has one, and roughly

00:05:34.913 --> 00:05:37.815
what using it would actually involve, before you're

00:05:37.815 --> 00:05:39.992
ever in a position where you'd need it.

00:05:41.292 --> 00:05:43.331
Here's how to actually check this for

00:05:43.331 --> 00:05:45.369
a rollup you already use, rather than

00:05:45.369 --> 00:05:47.547
assuming it works a particular way.

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:50.625
Search its own documentation specifically for the

00:05:50.625 --> 00:05:54.072
words sequencer, decentralisation, or escape hatch, and

00:05:54.072 --> 00:05:56.034
read what it actually says.

00:05:56.034 --> 00:05:58.424
Check whether it's ever published a real

00:05:58.424 --> 00:06:01.047
incident report from a sequencer outage, and

00:06:01.047 --> 00:06:03.504
how that outage was actually handled.

00:06:03.504 --> 00:06:06.038
And if you genuinely can't find a clear answer

00:06:06.038 --> 00:06:08.464
either way, treat that itself as the finding.

00:06:09.764 --> 00:06:10.854
Quick check.

00:06:10.854 --> 00:06:12.779
What exactly is an escape hatch?

00:06:17.199 --> 00:06:18.908
The answer: a way to force a

00:06:18.908 --> 00:06:21.973
transaction or withdrawal directly through the base

00:06:21.973 --> 00:06:24.389
chain, if the sequencer is censoring you.

00:06:25.689 --> 00:06:26.694
Worked example.

00:06:27.994 --> 00:06:30.776
Here's the actual choice, laid out side by

00:06:30.776 --> 00:06:34.334
side, for genuinely moving twenty thousand dollars off

00:06:34.334 --> 00:06:37.739
an optimistic rollup, back onto Ethereum itself.

00:06:37.739 --> 00:06:40.622
The native bridge is the safest path available,

00:06:40.622 --> 00:06:43.446
but takes roughly seven days, waiting out that

00:06:43.446 --> 00:06:46.749
same challenge window from earlier in this lesson.

00:06:46.749 --> 00:06:49.719
A fast bridge instead takes just minutes, for a

00:06:49.719 --> 00:06:53.122
fee, specifically by relying on that fast bridge's own

00:06:53.122 --> 00:06:57.329
available liquidity, rather than the rollup's own native settlement.

00:06:58.629 --> 00:07:01.248
Here's the one sentence this entire worked

00:07:01.248 --> 00:07:03.928
example exists to prove, worth holding onto

00:07:03.928 --> 00:07:06.664
more than the specific seven-day figure.

00:07:06.664 --> 00:07:10.265
Plan your own liquidity ladder around the slow, native

00:07:10.265 --> 00:07:14.062
path, deliberately, treating the fast bridge as a genuine

00:07:14.062 --> 00:07:18.122
option for genuine emergencies, never as your actual, default

00:07:18.122 --> 00:07:20.544
plan for accessing this money at all.

00:07:21.844 --> 00:07:24.862
Here's how this actually connects to your own liquidity

00:07:24.862 --> 00:07:28.449
ladder, the framework covered properly back in Module twelve.

00:07:28.449 --> 00:07:31.258
Anything you might genuinely need within roughly

00:07:31.258 --> 00:07:33.437
seven days shouldn't be sitting on an

00:07:33.437 --> 00:07:36.604
optimistic rollup's native withdrawal path at all.

00:07:36.604 --> 00:07:38.868
Anything you're comfortable waiting a week

00:07:38.868 --> 00:07:40.484
for can sit there safely.

00:07:40.484 --> 00:07:43.639
And the fast bridge stays reserved specifically for

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:46.370
the gap between those two, when real urgency

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.372
and real cost both apply at once.

00:07:49.673 --> 00:07:51.133
Here's your checklist.

00:07:51.133 --> 00:07:53.978
Do it for every layer two you actually use.

00:07:53.978 --> 00:07:57.310
Know the rollup type, optimistic or Z.K., and the

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.743
real native withdrawal time that comes with it.

00:08:00.743 --> 00:08:04.257
Know your sequencer's actual setup, and whether a genuine

00:08:04.257 --> 00:08:07.163
escape hatch exists if it ever censors you.

00:08:07.163 --> 00:08:09.927
And build your own liquidity plan assuming the

00:08:09.927 --> 00:08:12.338
slow path every time, never the fast one.

00:08:13.638 --> 00:08:14.883
Let's recap.

00:08:14.883 --> 00:08:18.498
A rollup executes off Ethereum, then settles back to

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:22.727
it; optimistic rollups wait out a roughly seven-day challenge

00:08:22.727 --> 00:08:26.983
window for native withdrawals, Z.K. rollups can be faster.

00:08:26.983 --> 00:08:30.028
A sequencer orders your transactions, often as

00:08:30.028 --> 00:08:32.360
a single operator today; if it goes

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:34.344
down, the chain may pause.

00:08:34.344 --> 00:08:36.373
An escape hatch is your backup exit

00:08:36.373 --> 00:08:39.434
through Ethereum itself, if you're ever censored.

00:08:39.434 --> 00:08:42.265
And the real plan is to build your liquidity

00:08:42.265 --> 00:08:45.662
ladder around the slow path, always, keeping the fast

00:08:45.662 --> 00:08:48.304
bridge as a genuine emergency option only.

00:08:49.604 --> 00:08:52.807
Worth connecting this back to the dependency map from

00:08:52.807 --> 00:08:56.014
this module's own Mastery Starter, one more time.

00:08:56.014 --> 00:08:58.702
Your layer two is another specific link in

00:08:58.702 --> 00:09:01.327
that same chain: its own rollup type, its

00:09:01.327 --> 00:09:04.264
own sequencer setup, and its own escape hatch,

00:09:04.264 --> 00:09:07.014
each one worth knowing before you ever need

00:09:07.014 --> 00:09:09.139
to rely on it under real pressure.

00:09:10.439 --> 00:09:12.801
Know your rollup type and withdrawal time,

00:09:12.801 --> 00:09:15.107
know your sequencer and escape hatch, and

00:09:15.107 --> 00:09:17.659
plan your liquidity around the slow path.

00:09:17.659 --> 00:09:23.199
Next up, Lesson 5.3: oracles, TWAPs & manipulation.
